{0 Machines and states}

All behaviour in Silica is in machines. A machine is a statechart: a set of
states, the events that each state accepts, and the commands that each step
gives to the host. A machine is a plain value. It does not run by itself: a
host (the browser, the server or the checker) runs it. See {!Silica.Machine}.

{1:states States and snapshots}

The states of a machine are the constructors of a GADT. The type index of a
constructor is the type of the data that the state carries. The constructors
must be constant: the data is not in the constructor, it goes next to it.

[Machine.Make] takes the module of the states and gives the functions that
build the machine. A snapshot is the value of the machine at one moment: one
state and its data, written [At (state, data)].

The example below is a field with a debounce. It has three states: [Empty],
[Typing], which carries the text, and [Settled], which carries the text that
stayed for half a second.

{@mlx file=examples/shared/doc_debounce.mlx[
open Silica

type event = Typed of string | Settle | Clear

module States = struct
  type _ t = Empty : unit t | Typing : string t | Settled : string t
end

module Debounce_machine = Machine.Make (States)

let typed = function
  | "" ->
      Debounce_machine.goto Empty ()
  | text ->
      Debounce_machine.reenter Typing text

let machine =
  Debounce_machine.make ~id:"debounce"
    ~init:(Debounce_machine.at Empty ())
    [
      Debounce_machine.state Empty "empty" ~to_:[ Typing ] (fun () -> function
        | Typed text ->
            typed text
        | Settle | Clear ->
            Debounce_machine.unhandled
        );
      Debounce_machine.state Typing "typing" ~to_:[ Empty; Settled ]
        ~after:[ (500, Settle) ]
        (fun text -> function
          | Typed text ->
              typed text
          | Settle ->
              Debounce_machine.goto Settled text
          | Clear ->
              Debounce_machine.unhandled
          );
      Debounce_machine.state Settled "settled" ~to_:[ Empty; Typing ]
        (fun _ -> function
        | Typed text ->
            typed text
        | Settle | Clear ->
            Debounce_machine.unhandled
        );
      Debounce_machine.always ~to_:[ Empty ] (function
        | Clear ->
            Debounce_machine.goto Empty ()
        | Typed _ | Settle ->
            Debounce_machine.unhandled
        );
    ]

let view (snapshot : Debounce_machine.snapshot) : event Node.t =
  let text, note =
    match snapshot with
    | Debounce_machine.At (Empty, ()) ->
        ("", "Type a word.")
    | Debounce_machine.At (Typing, text) ->
        (text, "Waiting for you to stop.")
    | Debounce_machine.At (Settled, text) ->
        (text, "You typed " ^ text ^ ".")
  in
  <div>
    <input aria_label="Word" value=text on_input=(fun text -> Typed text) />
    <button on_press=Clear disabled=(text = "")>"Clear"</button>
    <p>(Node.text note)</p>
  </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

{1:cases The table of cases}

[Debounce_machine.make ~id ~init cases] makes the machine. [id] names the
machine in the spec, in the checker and in the ids of timers and sources.
[init] is the first snapshot. [?start] gives commands that run once, before the
entry of [init].

Each state has one case, made with
[Debounce_machine.state state name handler]. The handler gets the data of the
state and the event, and gives an outcome. The options of a case:

{t
| Option | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| [to_] | The states that the handler can go to. Going to the current state needs no entry. |
| [entry], [exit] | Commands to run when the machine enters or leaves the state. |
| [after] | Timers, in milliseconds, with the event that each one raises. |
| [invoke] | Sources: Remote calls, Live subscriptions and held resources. |
| [final] | The machine stops here: a final state handles no event. |
}

Timers and sources belong to the state. They start when the machine enters
the state and stop when it leaves the state, so a timer or an answer of a left
state never arrives. See {!page-commands} for the sources.

[Debounce_machine.always ~to_ handler] is a handler that every state that is
not final tries after its own handler gives [unhandled]. In the example,
[Clear] works in every state.

A machine that the checker cannot reach is a bug. Two exceptions catch the
most common ones when the machine is made or stepped:

- {!Silica.Machine.Missing_case}: a state in [init] or in a [to_] list has no
  case. [Debounce_machine.make] raises it.
- {!Silica.Machine.Undeclared_target}: a handler went to a state that is not
  in the [to_] list of its case. The step raises it.

{@ocaml[
# let broken () =
    let open Doc_debounce in
    Debounce_machine.make ~id:"broken"
      ~init:(Debounce_machine.at States.Empty ())
      [
        Debounce_machine.state States.Empty "empty" ~to_:[ States.Typing ]
          Debounce_machine.ignore;
      ];;
val broken :
  unit -> (Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.snapshot, 'a) Silica.Machine.t =
  <fun>
# broken ();;
Exception: Silica.Machine.Missing_case {machine = "broken"; index = 1}.
]}

{1:outcomes Outcomes}

{t
| Outcome | Meaning |
|---------|---------|
| [Debounce_machine.goto state data] | Go to the state. To the current state, only the data changes: no exit, no entry, the timers and sources keep running. |
| [Debounce_machine.reenter state data] | Like [goto], but exit and enter again, also to the current state. Timers and sources start again. |
| [Debounce_machine.stay cmds] | Keep the snapshot and run the commands. |
| [Debounce_machine.unhandled] | This state does not handle the event. The [always] handlers get it next. |
}

[goto] and [reenter] take [?cmds], the commands of the step. A debounce is
[reenter] on a state with an [after] timer: each new text starts the timer
again, so the timer fires only after the last key.

{1:run Run a machine}

{!Silica.Machine.start} gives the first snapshot and the commands of the start.
{!Silica.Machine.send} gives the next snapshot, the commands and whether the
event was handled. {!Silica.Machine.active} lists the timers and sources that
the current state owns.

{@ocaml[
# let machine = Doc_debounce.machine;;
val machine :
  (Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.snapshot, Doc_debounce.event)
  Silica.Machine.t = <abstr>
# let snapshot, _ = Silica.Machine.start machine;;
val snapshot : Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.snapshot =
  Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.At (Doc_debounce.States.Empty, <poly>)
# let snapshot, commands, _ =
    Silica.Machine.send machine snapshot (Doc_debounce.Typed "oc");;
val snapshot : Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.snapshot =
  Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.At (Doc_debounce.States.Typing, <poly>)
val commands : Doc_debounce.event Silica.Cmd.host list =
  [Silica.Cmd.Start_timer
    {Silica.Cmd.id = "debounce/typing/after0"; ms = 500;
     event = Doc_debounce.Settle}]
# Silica.Machine.active machine snapshot;;
- : Silica.Machine.resource list =
[Silica.Machine.Timer
  {Silica.Machine.id = "debounce/typing/after0"; ms = 500}]
]}

A second text enters [Typing] again. The commands stop the old timer and start
a new one:

{@ocaml[
# let snapshot, commands, _ =
    Silica.Machine.send machine snapshot (Doc_debounce.Typed "ocaml");;
val snapshot : Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.snapshot =
  Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.At (Doc_debounce.States.Typing, <poly>)
val commands : Doc_debounce.event Silica.Cmd.host list =
  [Silica.Cmd.Stop "debounce/typing/after0";
   Silica.Cmd.Start_timer
    {Silica.Cmd.id = "debounce/typing/after0"; ms = 500;
     event = Doc_debounce.Settle}]
# let snapshot, _, _ = Silica.Machine.send machine snapshot Doc_debounce.Settle;;
val snapshot : Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.snapshot =
  Doc_debounce.Debounce_machine.At (Doc_debounce.States.Settled, <poly>)
# Silica.Machine.key machine snapshot;;
- : string = "settled"
]}

{!Silica.Machine.step} is like [send], and also tells a host whether the step
entered a state. {!Silica.Machine.map_snapshot} shows the same machine over
another type of snapshot, for example a plain record.

{1:spec The spec}

{!Silica.Machine.spec} reads the shape of a machine without running a handler:
its states, its edges (from the [to_] lists and [always]), its timers and its
sources. {!Silica.Spec.lint} finds states that no path reaches and states that
no edge leaves. The checker, the Quint export and the diagrams use the spec;
see {!page-checker}.

{@ocaml[
# (Silica.Machine.spec Doc_debounce.machine).edges;;
- : (string * string) list =
[("empty", "empty"); ("empty", "typing"); ("settled", "empty");
 ("settled", "typing"); ("typing", "empty"); ("typing", "settled");
 ("typing", "typing")]
# Silica.Spec.lint (Silica.Machine.spec Doc_debounce.machine);;
- : Silica.Spec.issue list = []
]}

